A Map is a key-value collection that accepts any key type, preserves insertion order, and provides clean methods. Plain objects only accept string and symbol keys and inherit prototype properties.

code:
const m = new Map();
m.set('user', 'Ana');
m.set(1, 'number key');
console.log(m.get('user'));  // Ana

real example:
Mapping response status codes to enums is safer in a Map because numeric keys behave predictably.

A Map supports set, get, has, delete, clear, and the size property. It is iterated with for...of and supports forEach.

code:
const m = new Map([['a', 1]]);
m.set('b', 2);
console.log(m.has('a'));  // true
console.log(m.size);      // 2
m.delete('a');

real example:
Tracking per-request assertions with a Map keyed by URL lets a test verify each endpoint result individually.

destructuring with for...of yields key-value pairs, and the keys, values, and entries methods return iterators.

code:
const m = new Map([['x', 1], ['y', 2]]);
for (const [key, value] of m) {
  console.log(key, value);
}

real example:
Iterating config maps in a test data setup prints each case name and payload for easy debugging of failures.

Choose a Map when keys are not strings, when inserts happen frequently, when insertion order matters, or when you need easy size and deletion semantics.

code:
const cache = new Map();
userIds.forEach((id, i) => cache.set(id, i));

real example:
Caching unique request timestamps by id benefits from Map's clean has and delete that objects lack natively.

A WeakMap only accepts object keys and holds weak references, so it does not prevent garbage collection. It has no size property and no iteration.

code:
const wm = new WeakMap();
let obj = { id: 1 };
wm.set(obj, 'payload');
obj = null;  // entry can be collected

real example:
WeakMap is useful for attaching private per-object state in libraries, but test code generally prefers a regular Map for inspectability.

A WeakSet holds only object values with weak references, meaning it cannot hold primitives, has no size or iteration, and allows garbage collection of its objects.

code:
const ws = new WeakSet();
let obj = {};
ws.add(obj);
console.log(ws.has(obj));  // true

real example:
Marking DOM elements as visited in memory-sensitive scrapers uses WeakSet so removed nodes free memory automatically.

Filter one array for values the other Set does not have, using the Set's fast has check as the predicate.

code:
const expected = new Set(['a', 'b']);
const actual = ['a'];
const missing = expected.values();
[...expected].filter(x => !actual.includes(x));  // ['b']

real example:
Comparing a required-fields Set against keys actually present in a response surfaces which fields are missing in assertions.

Both constructors accept an iterable: Map takes key-value pairs, and Set takes values directly.

code:
const m = new Map([['a', 1], ['b', 2]]);
const s = new Set(['red', 'green']);

real example:
Building collections from API fixture arrays in a single line keeps test setup compact and reviewable.
